### What version of the Codex App are you using (From “About Codex” dialog)?

26.930.51102 (build 13100), read from the installed application bundle metadata. Bundled Codex core: 0.160.0.

### What subscription do you have?

ChatGPT Business.

### What platform is your computer?

Output of `uname -mprs`:

```text
Darwin 25.6.0 arm64 arm
```

macOS 26.7.1 (25G241), Apple Silicon. Embedded Chromium: 154.0.8037.98; V8: 15.4.80.20.

### What issue are you seeing?

Opening an existing local conversation repeatedly freezes the desktop renderer. One renderer consumes approximately 100% of one CPU core, while the backend is idle. The confirmed failure is a CPU hang: no corresponding macOS crash report was found.

The triggering stored item is a tool-delivered notification, rendered through the user-message Markdown parser. Its unfenced JSON array contains embedded code fences alongside escaped newlines. The stored output is 16,936 characters; the decoded fragment passed to the failing regex is 14,675 characters, with 140 backticks and 22 triple-backtick runs.

This is an additional occurrence of the inline-link parser defect reported in #49490. This report adds a notification replay trigger unrelated to Chat-to-Work handoff, confirmation on 26.930.51102, and a second failure in the reference-link tokenizer after the first regex is bypassed.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

**Observed application reproduction:**

1. Open the affected local conversation, whose saved notification contains the problematic JSON, from the sidebar.
2. Backend history reads and `thread/resume` complete successfully.
3. The renderer hangs while displaying the stored notification JSON containing escaped code fences.
4. Reopening the original stored data in an isolated app profile reproduces the hang. A fresh synthetic end-to-end notification delivery has not been tested.

The projected history contains 29 turns and 1,546 items and uses paginated loading. Token/context-window utilization was not captured at the historical failure; the standalone parser reproduction below does not require a model request.

**Independent synthetic reproduction:** save the following as `reproduce-markdown-hang.mjs` and run `node reproduce-markdown-hang.mjs`. It uses the exact inline-link regex captured from the installed build, generated text only, and disposable workers with three-second timeouts.

````js
import { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } from 'node:worker_threads';
import { performance } from 'node:perf_hooks';

// Exact inline-link regex captured from Codex 26.930.51102.
// All test input is synthetic. Each slow test runs in a terminable worker.
const pattern = "^!?\\[((?:\\[(?:\\\\[\\s\\S]|[^\\[\\]\\\\])*\\]|\\\\[\\s\\S]|`+[^`]*?`+(?!`)|[^\\[\\]\\\\`])*?)\\]\\(\\s*(<(?:\\\\.|[^\\n<>\\\\])+>|[^ \\t\\n\\x00-\\x1f]*)(?:(?:[ \\t]*(?:\\n[ \\t]*)?)(\"(?:\\\\\"?|[^\"\\\\])*\"|'(?:\\\\'?|[^'\\\\])*'|\\((?:\\\\\\)?|[^)\\\\])*\\)))?\\s*\\)";

if (!isMainThread) {
  const regex = new RegExp(pattern);
  const start = performance.now();
  const matched = regex.test(workerData.input);
  parentPort.postMessage({ matched, elapsedMs: performance.now() - start });
} else {
  const timeoutMs = 3000;
  console.log('Codex Markdown link-label regex reproduction; worker timeout = 3000 ms');
  console.log('Input: "[" + "```x``` ".repeat(count) + "]"');
  for (const count of [4, 6, 8, 10, 12]) {
    const input = '[' + '```x``` '.repeat(count) + ']';
    const worker = new Worker(new URL(import.meta.url), { workerData: { input } });
    const result = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const timer = setTimeout(async () => {
        await worker.terminate();
        resolve({ timeoutMs });
      }, timeoutMs);
      worker.once('message', async value => {
        clearTimeout(timer);
        await worker.terminate();
        resolve(value);
      });
      worker.once('error', error => { clearTimeout(timer); reject(error); });
    });
    console.log(JSON.stringify({ count, characters: input.length, ...result }));
  }
}
````

The generated input is `'[' + '```x``` '.repeat(count) + ']'`.

Measured separately in workers using the installed app’s V8 engine:

| Repetitions | Characters | Result |
| ---: | ---: | --- |
| 4 | 34 | 0.1 ms, no match |
| 6 | 50 | 0.8 ms, no match |
| 8 | 66 | 23.3 ms, no match |
| 10 | 82 | 751.7 ms, no match |
| 12 | 98 | Exceeded 3,000 ms; worker terminated |

These are local diagnostic timings. The original 14,675-character fragment also exceeded a three-second limit when its captured regex was executed outside the app.

### What is the expected behavior?

Opening and displaying stored messages should perform bounded work and keep the UI responsive, including for malformed Markdown and serialized notification data. The 98-character synthetic case should complete promptly. Expensive parsing should have a reliable timeout and literal-text fallback rather than blocking the renderer main thread.

### Additional information

**Root cause:** catastrophic regex backtracking in the shared Markdown link-label pattern. The repeated label alternative contains:

````text
`+[^`]*?`+(?!`)
````

Opening and closing backtick runs can be partitioned in many ways within the repeated label expression. When no valid link ending is available, the regex explores those partitions. The JSON notification’s escaped newlines keep the embedded code fences in text reaching the inline parser.

**Exact hot paths**, using one-based positions in this installed build’s minified assets:

```text
ZT.link                 app-shared-9d148924be0b.js:252:908
Object.tokenizer        user-message-markdown-7702f4223d07.js:1:1495
 e.inlineTokens         app-shared-9d148924be0b.js:266:1123
 rE.lexer               app-shared-9d148924be0b.js:285:4687
 EQ                     app-shared-9d148924be0b.js:518:1331
 w1c                    app-initial-f9b16fbf8fc7.js:4702:56008
```

The `composerLink` extension calls the standard link tokenizer before its custom link handling. Instrumented `RegExp.exec` recorded a call on the notification JSON with no return before the diagnostic capture ended. Bypassing only that inline-link regex exposed another non-returning call on the same fragment in `ZT.reflink`, at `app-shared-9d148924be0b.js:252:1794`, using the same label subpattern. The failing reference-link regex source is:

````text
^!?\[((?:\[(?:\\[\s\S]|[^\[\]\\])*\]|\\[\s\S]|`+[^`]*?`+(?!`)|[^\[\]\\`])*?)\]\[((?!\s*\])(?:\\[\s\S]|[^\[\]\\])+)\]
````

**Controlled validation**, using a separate Codex home and Electron user-data directory with a copy of the affected chat:

- Original data reproduces the hang: roughly 100% renderer CPU, approximately 490 MB RSS, and debugger `Profiler.stop` requests time out. A native sample captured the same main-thread call chain in all 2,434 samples; stripped native/JIT symbols alone do not establish the JavaScript function attribution.
- Truncating stored strings to 1,000 or 8,000 characters allows the chat to open.
- Replacing only MCP result bodies does not fix the hang.
- Bypassing only the inline-link regex exposes the reference-link regex hang on the same notification.
- Keeping all original stored data and bypassing the shared backtick-containing label regex family on inputs over 8,000 characters allows the chat to open, show its latest response, and service debugger requests. A CPU profile then stops successfully. The runtime hook observed 491 bypasses. This was a temporary diagnostic intervention in the copied profile, not an installed fix.

Original application logs independently show successful history reads and a successful `thread/resume` immediately before the frontend stalls, including a 1,275 ms resume. No model turn was submitted during the isolated tests.

**Suggested fix/regression coverage:** replace ambiguous link-label matching with bounded, linear tokenization and cover inline links, reference links, and any other rule sharing this expression. Include the 98-character synthetic case and notification JSON containing escaped embedded code fences. Rendering structured tool notifications as literal text/code is an additional mitigation; a large-text cutoff alone is insufficient for the 98-character example.

Build commit: `ac1981864a36743eb0712eb0e3b03e57ca35cb81`.

Related existing report: https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/49490 (same inline-link defect on a prior build and a different entry path).

Only synthetic input, diagnostic asset names and aggregate results are included. No company-internal URLs, repository names or code, personal filesystem paths, credentials, conversation/thread/turn/item/account identifiers, raw notification bodies, workflow bindings or original transcript text are included.

Prepared with Codex (GPT-6.1 Sol).
